#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096311 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90455606-1080-552e-a825-0cc7054b4be3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "210f48cc-8812-564d-9dae-5a8a42165ef9" :question/math-academy-id "e-14005" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n${x + 2y =-4; x - \\frac{y}{6} = \\frac{5}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $6$.\n\n$$\n{x + 2y =-4; x - \\frac{y}{6} = \\frac{5}{2} \\mid \\mid \\cdot 6\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Let's solve for $x$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 2y &= -4 \\\\\nx &= -4 - 2y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $x=-4-2y$ into the second equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - y &= 15 \\\\\n6(-4 - 2y) - y &= 15 \\\\\n- 24 - 12y - y &= 15 \\\\\n- 24 - 13y &= 15 \\\\\n- 13y &= 39 \\\\\ny &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $x$, we substitute $y=-3$ into the equation $x=-4-2y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= -4 - 2y \\\\\n&=-4 - 2(-3) \\\\\n&=-4 + 6 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 2$ and $y = -3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096312 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c880ac-41a9-543f-b7a1-b4dfcc5de99d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "ff844be7-7d1e-5a1f-a4d6-1d4313f5fd2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14006" :question/problem "Find the solution to the following system of equations:\n\n${5a + \\frac{3b}{2} = 2; \\frac{4a}{3} + 2b = 0$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions, we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n{5a + \\frac{3b}{2} = 2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 2; \\frac{4a}{3} + 2b = 0 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 3\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-6b$ term that can cancel with the $6b$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{10a + 3b = 4 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2); 4a + 6b = 0\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $b$-variable, and allows us to solve for $a$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-20a - 6b &= -8 \\\\\n4a + 6b &= 0 \\\\\n(-20a + 4a) &= (-8 + 0) \\\\\n- 16a &= -8 \\\\\na &= \\frac{-8}{-16} \\\\\na &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the equation\n\n$$\n4a+6b=0\n$$\n\nto solve for $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4a + 6b &= 0 \\\\\n4(\\frac{1}{2}) + 6b &= 0 \\\\\n2 + 6b &= 0 \\\\\n6b &= -2 \\\\\nb &= -\\frac{2}{6} \\\\\nb &= -\\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{1}{2}, \\,b =-\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096317 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14370371-529b-5f25-9963-62c0cd9a172e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Elimination"}] :question/id #uuid "fb7a4815-45e8-5288-8e96-c3741f1a2097" :question/math-academy-id "e-14732" :question/problem "${-x - 5y =-1; \\frac{1}{4}x + 3y = 2$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $4$.\n\n$$\n{-x - 5y =-1; \\frac{1}{4}x + 3y = 2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 4\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $-x$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $x$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable, and allows us to solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x - 5y &= -1 \\\\\nx + 12y &= 8 \\\\\n7y &= 7 \\\\\ny &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=1$ into the equation $-x-5y=-1$ to solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x - 5y &= -1 \\\\\n- x - 5 \\cdot 1 &= -1 \\\\\n- x - 5 &= -1 \\\\\n- x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx=-4, \\, y=1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096318 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3fea-e4cf-5537-9a33-06b9d2b35c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations Using Elimination"}] :question/id #uuid "7f77dc95-b22e-51af-a8c2-ef60ae4f3098" :question/math-academy-id "e-14733" :question/problem "Find the solution to the following system of equations.\n${\\frac{5}{2}x + 2y = 3; \\frac{1}{6}x - \\frac{2}{3}y =-7$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $6$, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{5}{2}x + 2y = 3 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 2; \\frac{1}{6}x - \\frac{2}{3}y =-7 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 6\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. We can eliminate $y$ by adding the two equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x + 4y &= 6 \\\\\nx - 4y &= -42 \\\\\n(5x + x) &= (6 - 42) \\\\\n6x &= -36 \\\\\nx &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-6$ into the second equation to solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 4y &= -42 \\\\\n- 6 - 4y &= -42 \\\\\n- 4y &= -36 \\\\\ny &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=-6$ and $y=9$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7eb3fea-e4cf-5537-9a33-06b9d2b35c6b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations Using Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d58c9f6-d6c8-534d-bff5-8be1c9bdd492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d58c9f6-d6c8-534d-bff5-8be1c9bdd492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232403} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7987735-a6e2-518c-a1de-a5ef26b254e7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095056} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12ca6bca-2d8a-5010-8df2-0c06586035f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12ca6bca-2d8a-5010-8df2-0c06586035f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232402} :answer-field/id #uuid "f29b023e-063c-57f9-9180-b6fdb001845c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095057}] :question/id #uuid "4ad0b8d4-1ab9-5165-9ffa-778e033d3dec" :question/math-academy-id "q-216178" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n${-\\frac{5}{2}a + 3b =-6; \\frac{5}{3}a - \\frac{1}{3}b = 9$\n\n$a =$\n{{field-1}} $, b =$\n{{field-2}}"}]}
